@Service
public class OrderService {
    // Both writes must succeed together, or neither should persist
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        inventoryRepo.decrementStock(order.getItemId(), order.getQty());
        orderRepo.save(order);
        // If save() throws, decrementStock() is rolled back automatically
    }
}
```

You need a transaction whenever two or more writes must either all succeed or all fail as a single unit of work.

```sql
-- Higher levels prevent more anomalies at the cost of concurrency
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;

START TRANSACTION;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;  -- read 1
-- ... other work ...
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;  -- read 2: same value guaranteed
COMMIT;
```

READ UNCOMMITTED allows dirty reads; READ COMMITTED stops them; REPEATABLE READ also stops non-repeatable reads; SERIALIZABLE stops phantoms too.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    // Joins the caller's transaction (or starts one) - shared rollback
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
    public void pay(Order order) {
        charge(order);
        auditService.log(order);  // see below
    }
}

@Service
public class AuditService {
    // Always its own transaction: the log survives even if pay() rolls back
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void log(Order order) {
        auditRepo.save(new AuditEntry(order));
    }
}
```

REQUIRED shares one transaction (and one rollback); REQUIRES_NEW suspends the outer transaction and commits the inner work independently.
